readings.c2s3:
¶
'''versification''' (vėr″si-fi-kā′shon), ''n.'' [< F. ''versification'' = Sp. ''versificacion'' = Pg. ''versificação'' = It. ''versificazione'', < L. ''versificatio(n-)'', < ''versificare'', versify: see ''versify''.] The act, art, or practice of composing poetic verse; the construction or measure of verse or poetry; metrical composition.
{{smaller block|Donne alone . . . had your talent; but was not happy enough to arrive at your ''versification.''
{{right|''Dryden'', Essay on Satire.}}
Bad ''versification'' alone will certainly degrade and render disgustful the sublimest sentiments.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Poetry Distinguished from Other Writing.}}
The theory that ''versification'' is not an indispensable requisite of a poem seems to have become nearly obsolete in our time.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XIX. 259.}}}}

'''versificator''' (vėr′si-fi-kā-tor), ''n.'' [< F. ''versificateur'' = Sp. Pg. ''versificador'' = It. ''versificatore'', < L. ''versificator'', < ''versificare'', versify: see ''versify''.] A versifier. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|I must farther add that Statius, the best ''versificator'' next to Virgil, knew not how to design after him, though he had the model in his eye.
{{right|''Dryden'', Essay on Satire.}}
Alliteration and epithets, which with mechanical ''versificators'' are a mere artifice, . . . charm by their consonance when they rise out of the emotions of the true poet.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., II. 128.}}}}

'''versificatrix''' (vėr′si-fi-kā-triks), ''n.'' [< L. as if *''versificatrix'', fem. of ''versificator'': see ''versificator''.] A woman who makes verses. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|In 1784 Beattie, writing of Hannah More, says that Johnson “told me, with great solemnity, that she was ‘the most powerful ''versificatrix''’ in the English language.”}}

